  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Households supply factors and buy goods; firms buy factors and sell goods. The other three are parts of the flow, not the whole of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Saving leaves the domestic flow unspent, as do taxes and imports; investment, government spending and exports are injections.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The four factor incomes — wages for labour, rent for land, interest for capital and profit for enterprise.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    They are investment in stocks, so the expenditure approach still matches the output approach in that year.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    It is measured at constant prices, so a change in it reflects a change in output rather than a change in prices.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Population — the average output per person, which is why rapid population growth can hold it down.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    1 ÷ (1 − 0.75) = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4. The 1.33 figure comes from dividing by 0.75 rather than by the marginal propensity to save.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Both parties must want what the other has at the same moment — money removes that requirement.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A rise in prices means each naira buys less, so the value of money and the price level move in opposite directions.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Storing value is secondary. The medium of exchange is the primary function, and legal tender is a status rather than a function.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Loans and securities are what the bank owns; deposits are liabilities because the bank owes them back.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    It keeps ₦25,000 and lends the remaining ₦75,000; the multiplier of 4 then describes the whole banking system's expansion, not this single loan.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Only the central bank may put new notes and coins into circulation, which is why it is called the bank of issue.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Persuasion rather than rule. The other three are quantitative instruments with legal or market force behind them.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Costs push prices up from the supply side; the other three act through demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    It creates an asset that lasts for years; salaries, stationery and interest are consumed or paid within the year.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Equality of the two sides. A budget can balance while still borrowing to refinance maturing debt, so the fourth option is too strong.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Higher taxes and lower spending reduce demand, which is the fiscal answer to demand-pull inflation.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A constant rate. Progression rises with income and regression falls with it.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Income, profits and gains — the burden cannot be shifted. Taxes on goods and services are indirect.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Who finally bears it, which depends on the elasticities of demand and supply — not on who hands the money over.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Borrowing to consume leaves the repayment without the productive asset that would have helped to meet it.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Fewer resources per unit is absolute advantage; lower opportunity cost is comparative advantage, and the two need not coincide.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Comparative advantage is the basis of mutual gain — a country can gain even when it is absolutely worse at everything.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The price ratio has moved in the country's favour, so the same exports command more imports.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The accounts must balance, so a deficit is financed from reserves or by capital inflow — the other options act on the domestic economy, not the external accounts.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A service such as banking, insurance, shipping or tourism. It crosses the border without appearing in the customs record of goods.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Temporary protection while costs fall with experience — the difficulty being that the protection is rarely withdrawn on time.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    The four freedoms — goods, services, labour and capital. A common external tariff is what a customs union already has, and a single currency goes further still.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    A range of indicators, of which the HDI is the best-known composite. GDP alone is growth, not development.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Health is one of the three components, so a fall in life expectancy lowers the index directly.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Death rates fall first, which is why the fastest population growth occurs in the middle of the transition before birth rates follow.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Diversification gives work in the off-season; the other three act on demand, skills or credit rather than on the calendar.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Unemployed over labour force — the standard definition, which is why discouraged workers dropping out can lower the rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Skills and health raise output per worker, which is the engine of long-run growth.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Assessment and collection of federal taxes including company income tax and VAT.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    They are usable foreign exchange, so a fall in oil earnings does not immediately halt essential imports.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    It returns a share to the area of origin, which in Nigeria means the oil-producing states benefit most.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    8 ÷ 160 × 100 = 5 per cent. Reading the index points as the rate gives 8 per cent, which is the standard trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 7.66pt)[
    Revenue swings with a price the country cannot control, so budgets must be planned and saved against a price that will not hold.
  ]

]

